<BaseLayout
  title="About | Employable PM"
  description="Amir Baruch — PM for 20 years, couldn't code for 19 of them. Writing about AI workflows, coding agents, and staying employable the hands-on way."
>
  <p class="kicker"><span class="accent">~/about</span></p>
  <h1>Amir Baruch</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong>Backend dev at SAP, then product, then back to building.</strong>
      I've spent my career bouncing between coding and product, chasing
      whatever I was passionate about at the time. The sweet spot turned
      out to be the middle: technical enough to be dangerous.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's the whole thesis here. PMs don't need to become engineers,
      but they should be technical enough that nobody can bullshit them —
      and curious enough to build the thing instead of filing a ticket
      for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The contrarian view: everyone tells PMs to stay out of the code. I
      think that's backwards. My best product calls came from understanding
      what the machine can actually do.
    </p>
    <p>
      Day job: Director of Product Management (Geo, Maps &amp; Agentic AI)
      at Stellantis — mapping platforms across 12 automotive brands,
      leading a team of PMs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here: learning in public. Sometimes wrong in public too.
    </p>
